         <h2>The Honest Disadvantages of DIY Builds</h2>
         <p>
-          While the benefits of DIY battery storage are substantial, it is
-          crucial to acknowledge the disadvantages and challenges involved.
-          Going the DIY route is not merely a matter of swapping parts; it
-          requires a significant investment of time, knowledge, and effort.
-          First and foremost is the safety risk. Lithium and sodium-ion
-          batteries store large amounts of energy in a compact form. Mishandling
-          cells, improper wiring, or incorrect BMS configuration can lead to
-          short circuits, fires, or explosions. Unlike pre-built systems that
-          undergo rigorous factory testing and certification, a DIY build places
-          the responsibility for safety entirely on the builder. This includes
-          ensuring proper fusing, secure connections, and adequate ventilation.
+          <strong>No pack warranty.</strong> Cells carry their own limited
+          warranty; the assembled system is yours to debug. A BMS
+          misconfiguration or loose busbar is on you.
         </p>
         <p>
-          Time is another major factor. Sourcing components, testing cells,
-          designing the enclosure, wiring the busbars, programming the BMS, and
-          integrating with inverters can take weeks or even months for a novice.
-          This contrasts sharply with pre-built systems, which can be unboxed
-          and installed in hours. For individuals with limited free time, the
-          opportunity cost of a DIY project may outweigh the financial savings.
-          Additionally, there is no warranty. If a cell fails after six months,
-          you cannot return the entire pack to the manufacturer for a refund.
-          You must troubleshoot the issue yourself, replace the faulty cell, and
-          absorb the cost and inconvenience.
+          <strong>Your time has value.</strong> Research, sourcing,
+          top-balancing, assembly, and commissioning is a full weekend for a
+          first build — less once you have done one. Compare that time with the
+          cost of a ready-built pack; the DIY materials range does not include
+          your labor or electrical work.
         </p>
         <p>
-          BMS integration can also be technically challenging. Selecting a BMS
-          that matches your cell count, voltage range, and current requirements
-          requires careful calculation. Misconfiguring parameters such as
-          over-voltage, under-voltage, or charge/discharge currents can damage
-          cells or trigger false protections. Furthermore, busbar torque and
-          fusing are critical details that are often overlooked. Loose
