         <h2>Safety First: Non-Negotiable Rules</h2>
         <p>
-          Safety must be the absolute, non-negotiable priority in any DIY
-          battery storage project. Working with high-energy-density cells
-          carries inherent risks that range from electrical shock and arc flash
-          to thermal runaway and fire. To mitigate these dangers, you must
-          adhere to a strict set of operational protocols. First, always wear
-          appropriate personal protective equipment (PPE). This includes
-          ANSI-rated safety glasses to protect against electrolyte spray or
-          debris, insulated gloves rated for the voltage levels you are working
-          with, and flame-resistant clothing. Never work alone if possible;
-          having a partner nearby can assist in emergencies, such as
-          administering first aid or activating emergency shutdown procedures.
-          Keep your workspace clean, dry, and free of conductive materials like
-          jewelry or loose metal tools that could bridge terminals.
+          <strong>1. Fuse everything.</strong> A Class-T or ANL fuse on the
+          positive terminal, sized to your wire gauge, is mandatory — not
+          optional.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          <strong>2. Never skip the BMS.</strong> No cell-level monitoring means
+          no overcharge, over-discharge, or temperature protection. A $60–120
+          BMS is the cheapest insurance in the build.
         </p>
         <p>
-          Second, implement robust circuit protection at every stage. Every
-          positive terminal connection leading out of the battery pack should be
-          fused appropriately to prevent excessive current flow in case of a
-          short circuit. Use high-quality fuses or DC-rated circuit breakers
-          specifically designed to interrupt high-voltage DC arcs, as standard
-          AC-rated devices may fail catastrophically when handling DC loads.
-          Ensure all connections are tight and secure; loose connections
-          generate resistance, which leads to heat buildup, insulation melting,
-          and potential fire hazards. Use a calibrated torque wrench to tighten
-          busbar bolts to the manufacturer’s exact specifications, typically
-          between 5-7 Nm for prismatic cells, depending on the bolt size.
-          Regularly inspect connections for signs of corrosion or loosening over
-          time.
+          <strong>3. Torque and insulate.</strong> Loose busbars arc. Follow
+          manufacturer torque specs, use insulated tools, and keep a fire
+          extinguisher (Class D or ABC) within reach during commissioning.
         </p>
         <p>
-          Third, strictly isolate DC and AC sides within your enclosure. Keep
-          high-current DC wiring physically separated from low-voltage control
-          wiring and AC outputs to prevent electromagnetic interference and
-          accidental shorts. Use color-coded wires—red for positive, black or
-          blue for negative—to avoid confusion during assembly and maintenance.
-          Label all connections clearly with durable tags indicating voltage,
-          current ratings, and function. This clarity is vital for
-          troubleshooting and future upgrades. Ensure that your enclosure itself
-          is properly grounded and that all ventilation holes are covered with
-          mesh to prevent foreign object entry while allowing heat dissipation.
+          <strong>4. Commission outdoors first.</strong> First full
+          charge/discharge cycle outside living spaces, monitored. Most failures
+          show up in the first 48 hours.
         </p>
         <p>
-          Fourth, never attempt grid connection yourself unless you are a
-          licensed electrician. Grid-tie inverters require precise
-          synchronization and safety protocols, including anti-islanding
-          protection, to protect utility workers from live lines during outages.
-          Improper grid connection can cause severe injury, property damage, and
-          significant legal liabilities. Always consult local regulations and
-          hire a qualified professional for any work involving the main
-          electrical panel or grid interconnection. Finally, keep a Class D fire
-          extinguisher nearby when working with lithium or sodium-ion batteries,
-          as water-based extinguishers may be ineffective or dangerous due to
-          electrical conductivity and chemical reactions. Remember, this
-          information is educational; always verify safety practices with a
-          licensed professional before proceeding.
+          Verify every design with a licensed electrician before connecting
+          loads. Batteries store enough energy to start fires and weld tools —
+          respect that.
         </p>
 
